      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The stronger legal position is arguing that while expansive executive privilege poses a significant threat to the separation of powers, absolutely no national security or other interest justifies granting the President the power to withhold all documented communications designated for political use. This stance aligns with Supreme Court precedents that narrowly defined executive privilege to prevent a catastrophic imbalance, suggesting that such broad powers ultimately allow the presidency to conceal its own misconduct rather than protect it. You should focus your arguments on history showing excessive privilege has led to unnecessary stagnation and judicial intervention, proving transparency is more essential for effective governance than secrecy.",
